<ServiceLayout
  title="Estate Planning for Newlyweds | Newly Married | Mesa Estate Planning"
  description="Marriage changes how Arizona law treats your assets, your inheritance rights, and your decision-making authority. Estate planning for newly married couples in Mesa, AZ."
  heroTitle="Marriage changed your legal relationship to everything you own."
  heroSubtitle="Arizona is a community property state. That has real consequences for your assets, your inheritance, and what happens if one of you can't make decisions. A plan built for two protects both of you."
  breadcrumbParent="Life Stages"
  breadcrumbParentUrl="/life-stages"
  keywords="estate planning newlyweds arizona, community property estate planning, estate planning after marriage, newly married estate plan mesa az"
  relatedServices={[
    { title: 'Wills & Trusts', href: '/services/wills-and-trusts', desc: 'Revocable trusts and wills for married couples.' },
    { title: 'Powers of Attorney', href: '/services/financial-power-of-attorney', desc: 'Financial and healthcare decision-making authority.' },
    { title: 'Asset Protection', href: '/services/asset-protection', desc: 'Protect separate property and growing assets.' },
  ]}
>
  <div class="quick-answer">
    <div class="quick-answer-label">Quick Answer</div>
    <p>When you marry in Arizona, most assets acquired during the marriage become <a href="/glossary#community-property">community property</a> — owned equally by both spouses regardless of whose name is on the account. Your estate plan needs to reflect this reality. At minimum, newly married couples should have coordinated wills or a joint revocable trust, updated beneficiary designations on all accounts, healthcare directives for both spouses, and financial powers of attorney naming each other.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <p>Getting married is one of the most significant legal events in a person's life — and most couples don't realize how much it changes. In Arizona, marriage creates a community property regime that affects every asset you acquire from the date of marriage forward. It changes your inheritance rights, your tax filing status, your default beneficiaries, and the legal authority you have over each other's medical and financial decisions.</p>

    <p>Many newlyweds assume that being married means their spouse automatically inherits everything, can make all decisions, and is fully protected. That's partially true — but the gaps in that assumption are exactly where problems arise. A surviving spouse does have inheritance rights under Arizona law, but those rights may not match what you intended, especially if there are children from a prior relationship, separate property assets, or debts.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>What Newly Married Couples Need</h2>
    <p><strong>Coordinated Wills or Joint Revocable Trust.</strong> A <a href="/services/wills-and-trusts">revocable living trust</a> avoids probate and gives both spouses clear control over how assets are distributed. For couples with straightforward situations, coordinated wills may be sufficient. We help you determine which structure fits.</p>

    <p><strong>Beneficiary Designation Review.</strong> Your 401(k), IRA, life insurance, and bank accounts all have beneficiary designations that may still name a parent, sibling, or ex. These designations override your will or trust. Updating them is one of the most important — and most frequently overlooked — steps after marriage.</p>

    <p><strong>Healthcare Directives.</strong> Each spouse needs their own <a href="/services/healthcare-directive">healthcare directive</a> naming the other as primary healthcare agent. If your spouse is also incapacitated, a secondary agent should be named.</p>

    <p><strong>Financial Powers of Attorney.</strong> Each spouse should have a <a href="/services/financial-power-of-attorney">financial power of attorney</a> naming the other. This ensures either spouse can manage the other's separate property, access accounts, and handle financial obligations during incapacity.</p>

    <p><strong>Community Property Agreement (if applicable).</strong> Arizona allows spouses to agree in writing about how community property is characterized. If one spouse brought significant separate property into the marriage or will receive an inheritance, documenting the characterization of assets now prevents disputes later.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>Community Property: What It Means for You</h2>
    <p>Arizona is one of nine community property states. In general, any income earned and any asset acquired during the marriage belongs equally to both spouses — regardless of who earned it or whose name appears on the title. Property owned before the marriage, and gifts or inheritances received by one spouse during the marriage, remain that spouse's separate property — but only if they're kept separate.</p>

    <p>Commingling separate and community funds is one of the most common planning mistakes we see. Once separate property is mixed into a joint account, tracing it back becomes difficult and expensive. A plan that addresses property characterization early protects both spouses.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>What If We Already Have Plans From Before the Marriage?</h2>
    <p>If either spouse has an existing estate plan — from when they were single, or from a prior marriage — it needs to be reviewed immediately after the wedding. Documents that name a former partner as agent, trustee, or beneficiary don't automatically update when you marry. Arizona law revokes certain provisions in favor of a former spouse upon divorce, but does not automatically add your new spouse to anything.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>What It Costs</h2>
    <p>Estate planning for newly married couples is flat-fee, quoted after your <a href="/contact">free consultation</a>. Couples typically save by planning together rather than separately.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-section">
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          Does my spouse automatically inherit everything in Arizona?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Your spouse inherits your share of community property by default, but the rules for separate property are different. If you have separate property and no will, your surviving spouse shares it with your descendants or parents under Arizona intestacy law. A will or trust ensures your spouse inherits exactly what you intend — and nothing falls through the cracks.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          We don't have kids yet. Do we still need a trust?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>It depends on your assets and goals. A trust avoids probate, which in Arizona can take six months or more and becomes a public record. If you own a home, have retirement accounts, or want to keep your estate private, a trust is often worth it even without children. We'll discuss whether a trust or coordinated wills make more sense for your situation during the consultation.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          What about a prenup — is it too late?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>A prenuptial agreement must be signed before marriage. After the wedding, a postnuptial agreement can accomplish similar goals — particularly around characterizing separate vs. community property, or addressing how assets from a prior relationship are handled. If protecting separate property is a concern, we address it as part of your estate plan.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          Should we update our beneficiary designations right after the wedding?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Yes — and it should be one of the first things you do. Beneficiary designations on retirement accounts, life insurance policies, and bank accounts override your will or trust. If your 401(k) still names a parent or former partner as beneficiary, that person receives the funds regardless of what your will says. We provide a beneficiary audit checklist as part of every engagement.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <script>
    function toggleFaq(btn) {
      const item = btn.closest('.faq-item');
      const isOpen = item.classList.contains('open');
      document.querySelectorAll('.faq-item.open').forEach(el => el.classList.remove('open'));
      if (!isOpen) item.classList.add('open');
    }
  </script>
</ServiceLayout>
